1
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:12,560
Christmas Eve,

2
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:13,440
1914

3
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:16,040
The First World War is only five months old

4
00:00:16,449 --> 00:00:18,319
Along the front lines in Belgium,

5
00:00:18,319 --> 00:00:20,479
British and German soldiers face each other

6
00:00:20,479 --> 00:00:21,962
across frozen mud

7
00:00:22,112 --> 00:00:23,022
It is bitterly cold,

8
00:00:23,022 --> 00:00:24,702
and many of them are far from home for

9
00:00:24,702 --> 00:00:26,131
the very first time

10
00:00:26,281 --> 00:00:27,791
They have spent weeks trying to kill each

11
00:00:27,791 --> 00:00:28,451
other

12
00:00:28,601 --> 00:00:29,231
But that night,

13
00:00:29,231 --> 00:00:30,991
something unexpected happens

14
00:00:31,454 --> 00:00:32,244
The shooting stops

15
00:00:32,244 --> 00:00:34,350
And the enemies begin to sing

16
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,100
This is the true story of the Christmas Truce

17
00:00:40,817 --> 00:00:43,687
When the war began in the summer of 1914,

18
00:00:43,687 --> 00:00:45,647
many people believed it would be over by

19
00:00:45,647 --> 00:00:46,767
Christmas

20
00:00:47,064 --> 00:00:48,694
Britain joined the war in August,

21
00:00:48,694 --> 00:00:50,374
to defend Belgium

22
00:00:50,524 --> 00:00:51,834
Young men marched off cheering,

23
00:00:51,834 --> 00:00:53,927
with flowers in their caps

24
00:00:54,077 --> 00:00:55,867
Many had never left their home towns before

25
00:00:55,867 --> 00:00:56,387
For some,

26
00:00:56,387 --> 00:00:58,734
it felt like a great adventure

27
00:00:58,884 --> 00:01:01,045
But the fighting quickly got stuck

28
00:01:01,195 --> 00:01:03,225
Huge battles in France and Belgium left

29
00:01:03,225 --> 00:01:05,825
hundreds of thousands of men killed or wounded

30
00:01:06,316 --> 00:01:08,746
Both sides dug long lines of trenches across

31
00:01:08,746 --> 00:01:10,506
Belgium and France

32
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:11,730
To survive the guns,

33
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:14,330
soldiers had to dig deep into the ground

34
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:16,110
They stretched for hundreds of miles,

35
00:01:16,110 --> 00:01:18,710
from the sea all the way to Switzerland

36
00:01:19,053 --> 00:01:19,603
By December,

37
00:01:19,603 --> 00:01:22,203
nobody was going home for Christmas

38
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:28,208
Life in the trenches was miserable

39
00:01:28,358 --> 00:01:30,348
Many soldiers suffered from trench foot,

40
00:01:30,348 --> 00:01:32,948
from standing in cold water for days

41
00:01:33,189 --> 00:01:34,179
It was cold and wet,

42
00:01:34,179 --> 00:01:35,419
and the mud was everywhere

43
00:01:35,419 --> 00:01:37,695
Rats ran through the dugouts

44
00:01:37,845 --> 00:01:38,995
Lice crawled in their clothes,

45
00:01:38,995 --> 00:01:40,981
and they could rarely wash

46
00:01:41,131 --> 00:01:43,041
Between the two lines was a strip of torn-up

47
00:01:43,041 --> 00:01:45,347
ground called No Man's Land

48
00:01:45,497 --> 00:01:46,847
It was full of barbed wire,

49
00:01:46,847 --> 00:01:48,865
shell holes and danger

50
00:01:49,015 --> 00:01:49,365
At night,

51
00:01:49,365 --> 00:01:51,765
soldiers crawled out to repair the barbed wire,

52
00:01:51,765 --> 00:01:53,753
hoping no one would see them

53
00:01:53,903 --> 00:01:54,653
In some places,

54
00:01:54,653 --> 00:01:57,253
the enemy trenches were only fifty meters away

55
00:01:58,095 --> 00:02:00,205
Snipers watched for anyone who raised their

56
00:02:00,205 --> 00:02:01,638
head too high

57
00:02:01,788 --> 00:02:03,778
So close that soldiers could hear each other

58
00:02:03,778 --> 00:02:04,898
talking

59
00:02:05,143 --> 00:02:06,853
British soldiers were called Tommies

60
00:02:06,853 --> 00:02:09,453
The Germans were often called Fritz

61
00:02:13,050 --> 00:02:14,600
As Christmas came closer,

62
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:16,680
parcels arrived from home

63
00:02:16,951 --> 00:02:18,221
Families sent socks,

64
00:02:18,221 --> 00:02:18,901
scarves,

65
00:02:18,901 --> 00:02:20,661
gloves and cake

66
00:02:20,830 --> 00:02:23,180
British soldiers received small brass tins from

67
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:23,980
Princess Mary,

68
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:25,626
filled with treats

69
00:02:25,776 --> 00:02:27,686
Inside were tobacco or sweets,

70
00:02:27,686 --> 00:02:28,686
a Christmas card,

71
00:02:28,686 --> 00:02:30,863
and a picture of the princess

72
00:02:31,013 --> 00:02:33,003
German soldiers were sent thousands of small

73
00:02:33,003 --> 00:02:34,443
Christmas trees

74
00:02:34,694 --> 00:02:36,764
Every soldier also got a Christmas card from

75
00:02:36,764 --> 00:02:38,457
the King and Queen

76
00:02:38,607 --> 00:02:40,757
Even the Pope asked the armies to stop fighting

77
00:02:40,757 --> 00:02:42,080
for Christmas

78
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:43,820
He hoped the guns would fall silent,

79
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:46,225
at least on the night the angels sang

80
00:02:46,375 --> 00:02:47,445
But the generals said no

81
00:02:47,445 --> 00:02:49,382
The war would go on

82
00:02:49,532 --> 00:02:51,682
The soldiers expected a Christmas like every

83
00:02:51,682 --> 00:02:54,082
other day of the war

84
00:02:57,217 --> 00:02:58,087
On Christmas Eve,

85
00:02:58,087 --> 00:03:00,247
British soldiers saw lights appearing along the

86
00:03:00,247 --> 00:03:01,687
German trenches

87
00:03:01,978 --> 00:03:02,888
The rain had stopped,

88
00:03:02,888 --> 00:03:05,276
and a hard frost covered the ground

89
00:03:05,426 --> 00:03:05,896
At first,

90
00:03:05,896 --> 00:03:07,819
they thought it was a trick

91
00:03:07,969 --> 00:03:09,679
Officers warned their men to stay alert

92
00:03:09,679 --> 00:03:11,767
It could be the start of an attack

93
00:03:11,917 --> 00:03:12,907
But the lights were candles,

94
00:03:12,907 --> 00:03:15,227
on small Christmas trees placed along the top

95
00:03:15,227 --> 00:03:16,540
of the trench

96
00:03:16,690 --> 00:03:19,160
Small paper lanterns and candles glowed in the

97
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:19,680
darkness,

98
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:21,532
all along the line

99
00:03:21,682 --> 00:03:22,552
Then they heard singing,

100
00:03:22,552 --> 00:03:24,952
drifting across No Man's Land

101
00:03:25,154 --> 00:03:27,104
The sound carried clearly through the still,

102
00:03:27,104 --> 00:03:28,476
cold air

103
00:03:28,626 --> 00:03:29,666
It was a German carol,

104
00:03:29,666 --> 00:03:30,896
Stille Nacht

105
00:03:30,896 --> 00:03:32,336
Silent Night

106
00:03:32,643 --> 00:03:34,193
Many British soldiers knew the tune,

107
00:03:34,193 --> 00:03:36,139
and listened in silence

108
00:03:36,289 --> 00:03:38,319
Some German soldiers had worked in Britain

109
00:03:38,319 --> 00:03:39,039
before the war,

110
00:03:39,039 --> 00:03:41,439
and called out in English

111
00:03:44,717 --> 00:03:45,987
The British soldiers listened

112
00:03:45,987 --> 00:03:47,389
Then they clapped

113
00:03:47,539 --> 00:03:49,317
The Germans cheered back

114
00:03:49,467 --> 00:03:52,045
And they started singing carols of their own

115
00:03:52,195 --> 00:03:53,105
Songs like O Come,

116
00:03:53,105 --> 00:03:55,305
All Ye Faithful were sung by both sides,

117
00:03:55,305 --> 00:03:57,294
in English and in Latin

118
00:03:57,444 --> 00:03:57,794
Soon,

119
00:03:57,794 --> 00:03:59,634
the two sides were taking turns,

120
00:03:59,634 --> 00:04:01,674
singing back and forth across the frozen

121
00:04:01,674 --> 00:04:02,704
ground

122
00:04:02,854 --> 00:04:04,764
Some soldiers held up signs saying:

123
00:04:04,764 --> 00:04:06,204
Merry Christmas

124
00:04:06,524 --> 00:04:07,914
Voices called out in the dark:

125
00:04:07,914 --> 00:04:08,754
Merry Christmas,

126
00:04:08,754 --> 00:04:09,154
Tommy!

127
00:04:09,154 --> 00:04:10,074
Merry Christmas,

128
00:04:10,074 --> 00:04:11,194
Fritz!

129
00:04:11,493 --> 00:04:12,363
In some places,

130
00:04:12,363 --> 00:04:13,243
signs read:

131
00:04:13,243 --> 00:04:14,043
You no fight,

132
00:04:14,043 --> 00:04:15,523
we no fight

133
00:04:15,673 --> 00:04:16,783
For the first time in weeks,

134
00:04:16,783 --> 00:04:18,762
the guns were silent

135
00:04:18,912 --> 00:04:19,662
In other places,

136
00:04:19,662 --> 00:04:21,942
officers met in No Man's Land that night,

137
00:04:21,942 --> 00:04:24,542
and agreed to a short truce

138
00:04:27,583 --> 00:04:28,413
On Christmas morning,

139
00:04:28,413 --> 00:04:30,333
a few brave soldiers climbed out of their

140
00:04:30,333 --> 00:04:31,453
trenches

141
00:04:31,705 --> 00:04:32,775
Everyone held their breath

142
00:04:32,775 --> 00:04:35,155
One shot could start the fighting again

143
00:04:35,305 --> 00:04:37,095
They walked into No Man's Land without their

144
00:04:37,095 --> 00:04:37,615
weapons,

145
00:04:37,615 --> 00:04:39,509
holding up their hands

146
00:04:39,659 --> 00:04:41,129
Others waved from the trenches,

147
00:04:41,129 --> 00:04:43,236
calling to them to come over

148
00:04:43,386 --> 00:04:43,976
Slowly,

149
00:04:43,976 --> 00:04:44,736
men stood up,

150
00:04:44,736 --> 00:04:46,766
and climbed out to meet them

151
00:04:46,916 --> 00:04:48,356
Nobody fired

152
00:04:48,646 --> 00:04:50,036
The two sides met in the middle,

153
00:04:50,036 --> 00:04:50,796
a little nervous,

154
00:04:50,796 --> 00:04:52,328
and a little shy

155
00:04:52,478 --> 00:04:53,868
More and more men followed,

156
00:04:53,868 --> 00:04:55,628
from both sides

157
00:04:55,787 --> 00:04:57,257
Many Germans spoke some English,

158
00:04:57,257 --> 00:04:59,857
and the men talked with words and gestures

159
00:05:00,223 --> 00:05:00,612
Soon,

160
00:05:00,612 --> 00:05:02,452
hundreds of enemy soldiers were standing

161
00:05:02,452 --> 00:05:03,012
together,

162
00:05:03,012 --> 00:05:04,452
shaking hands

163
00:05:04,854 --> 00:05:06,884
Officers from both sides joined in,

164
00:05:06,884 --> 00:05:08,644
saluting each other

165
00:05:12,183 --> 00:05:14,783
Then the soldiers started swapping gifts

166
00:05:14,982 --> 00:05:17,332
British soldiers gave away jam and corned beef

167
00:05:17,332 --> 00:05:19,932
The Germans offered beer and schnapps

168
00:05:20,207 --> 00:05:20,717
Chocolate,

169
00:05:20,717 --> 00:05:21,477
cigarettes,

170
00:05:21,477 --> 00:05:22,237
sausages,

171
00:05:22,237 --> 00:05:22,957
plum pudding,

172
00:05:22,957 --> 00:05:25,305
and buttons from their uniforms

173
00:05:25,455 --> 00:05:26,485
Some swapped caps,

174
00:05:26,485 --> 00:05:28,394
to keep as souvenirs

175
00:05:28,544 --> 00:05:30,014
They showed each other photos of their

176
00:05:30,014 --> 00:05:30,494
families,

177
00:05:30,494 --> 00:05:31,774
and wrote down addresses,

178
00:05:31,774 --> 00:05:33,944
promising to meet after the war

179
00:05:34,094 --> 00:05:35,204
Many of them were fathers,

180
00:05:35,204 --> 00:05:37,567
missing their own children at home

181
00:05:37,717 --> 00:05:38,427
In one place,

182
00:05:38,427 --> 00:05:40,667
a German soldier who had been a barber before

183
00:05:40,667 --> 00:05:43,221
the war gave British soldiers haircuts

184
00:05:43,371 --> 00:05:44,201
Others laughed,

185
00:05:44,201 --> 00:05:45,161
sang songs,

186
00:05:45,161 --> 00:05:47,241
and took photographs together

187
00:05:47,551 --> 00:05:48,701
Just one day before,

188
00:05:48,701 --> 00:05:50,617
they had been enemies

189
00:05:50,837 --> 00:05:52,597
Now they were sharing Christmas in the middle

190
00:05:52,597 --> 00:05:54,357
of a battlefield

191
00:05:57,783 --> 00:05:58,533
And in some places,

192
00:05:58,533 --> 00:06:00,292
a football appeared

193
00:06:00,442 --> 00:06:02,192
Nobody knows exactly where it started,

194
00:06:02,192 --> 00:06:04,312
but soldiers wrote about it in their letters

195
00:06:04,312 --> 00:06:05,192
home

196
00:06:05,342 --> 00:06:06,972
Soldiers started kicking it around on the

197
00:06:06,972 --> 00:06:07,412
frozen,

198
00:06:07,412 --> 00:06:08,792
muddy ground

199
00:06:08,942 --> 00:06:11,212
Officers and privates played side by side,

200
00:06:11,212 --> 00:06:13,715
in heavy boots and thick coats

201
00:06:13,865 --> 00:06:16,465
Caps and helmets were used as goalposts

202
00:06:16,756 --> 00:06:18,426
One German officer wrote that the British

203
00:06:18,426 --> 00:06:19,786
brought a ball from their trench,

204
00:06:19,786 --> 00:06:21,425
and a game began

205
00:06:21,575 --> 00:06:22,245
In some games,

206
00:06:22,245 --> 00:06:24,845
dozens of men chased the ball at once

207
00:06:25,059 --> 00:06:26,169
There were no real teams,

208
00:06:26,169 --> 00:06:28,171
and nobody kept score

209
00:06:28,321 --> 00:06:30,071
Some players slipped and fell in the mud,

210
00:06:30,071 --> 00:06:31,806
and everyone laughed

211
00:06:31,956 --> 00:06:32,706
Just young men,

212
00:06:32,706 --> 00:06:33,506
far from home,

213
00:06:33,506 --> 00:06:36,106
playing a game together on Christmas Day

214
00:06:36,507 --> 00:06:37,457
For a few hours,

215
00:06:37,457 --> 00:06:40,057
there was no war at all

216
00:06:42,950 --> 00:06:43,980
The truce was not planned

217
00:06:43,980 --> 00:06:45,740
It started with ordinary soldiers,

218
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:47,944
in many places along the line

219
00:06:48,094 --> 00:06:50,164
It happened mostly between British and German

220
00:06:50,164 --> 00:06:50,684
troops,

221
00:06:50,684 --> 00:06:52,983
near the Belgian town of Ypres

222
00:06:53,133 --> 00:06:54,363
There was no single truce,

223
00:06:54,363 --> 00:06:55,603
but many small ones,

224
00:06:55,603 --> 00:06:57,686
each starting in its own way

225
00:06:57,836 --> 00:06:59,866
Historians think around one hundred thousand

226
00:06:59,866 --> 00:07:01,390
men took part

227
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:04,010
French and Belgian soldiers took part in a few

228
00:07:04,010 --> 00:07:05,372
places too

229
00:07:05,522 --> 00:07:06,272
In some places,

230
00:07:06,272 --> 00:07:07,872
it lasted only a few hours

231
00:07:07,872 --> 00:07:08,432
In others,

232
00:07:08,432 --> 00:07:10,702
it lasted until the New Year

233
00:07:10,852 --> 00:07:11,562
In one place,

234
00:07:11,562 --> 00:07:13,602
chaplains from both sides held a service

235
00:07:13,602 --> 00:07:14,122
together,

236
00:07:14,122 --> 00:07:16,722
reading a psalm in English and German

237
00:07:16,890 --> 00:07:19,160
Both sides also used the quiet time to bury

238
00:07:19,160 --> 00:07:20,280
their fallen friends,

239
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:22,360
sometimes side by side

240
00:07:22,649 --> 00:07:23,239
Back home,

241
00:07:23,239 --> 00:07:25,239
newspapers printed soldiers' letters,

242
00:07:25,239 --> 00:07:27,387
and even photos of the truce

243
00:07:27,537 --> 00:07:29,167
It was a moment of peace in the middle

244
00:07:29,167 --> 00:07:30,997
of a terrible war

245
00:07:31,147 --> 00:07:32,697
Many soldiers wrote that they would never

246
00:07:32,697 --> 00:07:34,137
forget it

247
00:07:37,483 --> 00:07:39,153
When the generals heard about the truce,

248
00:07:39,153 --> 00:07:40,689
they were furious

249
00:07:40,839 --> 00:07:41,109
To them,

250
00:07:41,109 --> 00:07:43,709
the soldiers had forgotten who the enemy was

251
00:07:44,195 --> 00:07:45,865
They worried that soldiers who made friends

252
00:07:45,865 --> 00:07:48,097
with the enemy would refuse to fight

253
00:07:48,247 --> 00:07:50,397
Some officers feared the men would start asking

254
00:07:50,397 --> 00:07:52,300
why they were fighting at all

255
00:07:52,450 --> 00:07:53,320
New orders were sent

256
00:07:53,320 --> 00:07:55,830
Making friends with the enemy was forbidden

257
00:07:55,980 --> 00:07:56,770
In some places,

258
00:07:56,770 --> 00:07:58,970
soldiers fired warning shots into the air,

259
00:07:58,970 --> 00:08:01,570
to tell their new friends the truce was over

260
00:08:01,751 --> 00:08:03,957
Soldiers who did it could be punished

261
00:08:04,107 --> 00:08:05,977
Units that had made friends were sometimes

262
00:08:05,977 --> 00:08:08,068
moved to other parts of the front

263
00:08:08,218 --> 00:08:09,928
Officers who had joined in were warned they

264
00:08:09,928 --> 00:08:11,772
could face serious trouble

265
00:08:11,922 --> 00:08:12,232
Soon,

266
00:08:12,232 --> 00:08:14,312
the shooting started again

267
00:08:14,638 --> 00:08:15,348
Within days,

268
00:08:15,348 --> 00:08:16,388
the war was back,

269
00:08:16,388 --> 00:08:18,788
as if nothing had happened

270
00:08:21,983 --> 00:08:22,853
The next Christmas,

271
00:08:22,853 --> 00:08:24,253
in 1915,

272
00:08:24,253 --> 00:08:26,853
there were only a few small truces

273
00:08:27,080 --> 00:08:28,710
The commanders ordered heavy shooting on

274
00:08:28,710 --> 00:08:29,510
Christmas Eve,

275
00:08:29,510 --> 00:08:31,459
to stop any friendship

276
00:08:31,609 --> 00:08:34,479
The officers made sure nothing like 1914 would

277
00:08:34,479 --> 00:08:35,848
happen again

278
00:08:35,998 --> 00:08:36,988
As the war went on,

279
00:08:36,988 --> 00:08:38,548
it became more and more bitter,

280
00:08:38,548 --> 00:08:41,148
with poison gas and huge battles

281
00:08:41,374 --> 00:08:42,724
In 1916,

282
00:08:42,724 --> 00:08:45,044
after the huge battles of Verdun and the Somme,

283
00:08:45,044 --> 00:08:47,563
there were almost no truces at all

284
00:08:47,713 --> 00:08:50,313
The war went on for almost four more years

285
00:08:50,616 --> 00:08:53,035
It finally ended on November 11th,

286
00:08:53,035 --> 00:08:54,155
1918

287
00:08:54,669 --> 00:08:56,667
Millions of soldiers died

288
00:08:56,817 --> 00:08:59,417
Around nine million soldiers lost their lives

289
00:09:00,075 --> 00:09:02,075
Many of the men who shook hands that Christmas

290
00:09:02,075 --> 00:09:03,857
never made it home

291
00:09:04,007 --> 00:09:05,797
But those who survived never forgot that

292
00:09:05,797 --> 00:09:06,917
Christmas

293
00:09:10,583 --> 00:09:10,933
Today,

294
00:09:10,933 --> 00:09:12,973
the Christmas Truce is remembered as one of the

295
00:09:12,973 --> 00:09:15,194
most human moments of the war

296
00:09:15,344 --> 00:09:16,654
It has been told in books,

297
00:09:16,654 --> 00:09:17,214
songs,

298
00:09:17,214 --> 00:09:17,774
films,

299
00:09:17,774 --> 00:09:19,854
and even TV adverts

300
00:09:20,139 --> 00:09:21,689
Memorials stand in Belgium,

301
00:09:21,689 --> 00:09:23,716
near where the soldiers met

302
00:09:23,866 --> 00:09:27,176
A football memorial was unveiled there in 2014,

303
00:09:27,176 --> 00:09:29,243
for the hundredth anniversary

304
00:09:29,393 --> 00:09:29,783
Every year,

305
00:09:29,783 --> 00:09:31,143
visitors leave footballs,

306
00:09:31,143 --> 00:09:33,743
crosses and poppies at the memorial

307
00:09:34,015 --> 00:09:35,365
In 2014,

308
00:09:35,365 --> 00:09:36,765
one hundred years later,

309
00:09:36,765 --> 00:09:39,125
the British and German armies played a football

310
00:09:39,125 --> 00:09:40,924
match to remember it

311
00:09:41,074 --> 00:09:41,864
The British Army won,

312
00:09:41,864 --> 00:09:43,827
one goal to nil

313
00:09:43,977 --> 00:09:45,607
It showed that even in the middle of a

314
00:09:45,607 --> 00:09:45,927
war,

315
00:09:45,927 --> 00:09:47,984
people can choose kindness

316
00:09:48,134 --> 00:09:50,244
Ordinary people on both sides found they had

317
00:09:50,244 --> 00:09:52,454
much more in common than they thought

318
00:09:52,604 --> 00:09:54,434
Soldiers' letters are kept in museums,

319
00:09:54,434 --> 00:09:56,960
so we can still read their words today

320
00:09:57,110 --> 00:09:58,139
For one silent night,

321
00:09:58,139 --> 00:09:59,899
enemies chose peace

322
00:10:00,883 --> 00:10:01,793
One silent night

323
00:10:01,793 --> 00:10:02,913
Two enemy armies

324
00:10:02,913 --> 00:10:04,565
One game of football

325
00:10:04,715 --> 00:10:05,305
No orders

326
00:10:05,305 --> 00:10:05,865
No plan

327
00:10:05,865 --> 00:10:07,705
Just people choosing peace

328
00:10:07,855 --> 00:10:09,749
Would you have climbed out of the trench?

329
00:10:09,899 --> 00:10:11,364
Tell me in the comments

330
00:10:11,514 --> 00:10:14,114
Subscribe so you don't miss the next episode
